---
/**
 * Press — /press
 *
 * The honest brief for funders and press. Closes door 9 on /who-this-is-for.
 * Every claim is checked against about.astro, family.astro, contribute, and the
 * systems catalogue — the same anti-overclaim discipline as /teaching. Hard
 * lines: the catalogue figure is framed as "catalogued", never "supported"
 * (only four platforms are teachable today); no fixed unit/game counts; no
 * claim of real-hardware testing (suspended); no invented quotes or metrics.
 * House idiom: mono eyebrows, script headings, 3px borders, chunky offset
 * shadows, square corners.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { getAllPlatforms } from '../lib/platforms';

// Derived, not hard-coded — keeps the figures honest as the catalogue grows.
const allPlatforms = await getAllPlatforms();
const machineCount = allPlatforms.length;
const launch = allPlatforms
  .filter((p) => p.data.tier === 'live')
  .sort((a, b) => a.data.year - b.data.year);

const ORG = 'https://github.com/code198x';
const DISCORD = 'https://discord.gg/AdmEYQKWZb';
const PATREON = 'https://www.patreon.com/c/code_like_its_198x';

const facts = [
  [`${machineCount}`, 'machines catalogued — mapped, classified, and queued for the curriculum'],
  ['4', 'platforms with complete on-ramps you can learn from today'],
  ['100%', 'free and open source — no ads, no paywall, no account'],
  ['1', 'person building it, in the open, with AI as scaffolding'],
];
---

<Layout
  title="Press"
  description="The honest brief for funders and press. Code Like It's 198x teaches people how computers really work by building complete games on vintage machines — free, open source, built in the open by one person. What's real today, what the roadmap holds, and how to reach us."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="vhead">
      <p class="eyebrow">For funders and press</p>
      <h1>The honest brief.</h1>
      <div class="herorule"></div>
      <p class="lede">Code Like It's 198x teaches people <span class="mk">how computers really work</span>{' '}
        by building complete, finishable games on vintage machines. One person, an enormous ambition,
        built entirely in the open. Here's what's real today, what the roadmap holds, and where the
        line between the two sits — drawn honestly, because that's the whole point.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ============ THE ONE-LINER + WHY ============ -->
    <section class="band">
      <p class="aud">What it is</p>
      <h2>A curriculum that teaches the machine, not the framework</h2>
      <p class="intro">Most learning-to-code resources teach a language or a library. This one teaches
        the computer underneath — memory maps, cycle timing, sprites, sound chips — by having you build
        real games on the machines that made those ideas legible. The games are the sugar; the subject
        is computational thinking that outlasts any framework.</p>
      <p class="intro">The deeper aim is preservation. The people who built these systems are retiring,
        and the <em>why</em> behind their work is evaporating faster than the code. The curriculum
        archives the mental models — and the <a href="/vault">Vault</a> sets every machine, person and
        game against the world that produced it.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ SCALE + AMBITION ============ -->
    <section class="band alt">
      <p class="aud">Scale and ambition</p>
      <h2>Catalogued wide, taught deep</h2>
      <p class="intro">The ambition spans the 8-bit, 16-bit and 32-bit eras, across home computers and
        consoles, and across computing scenes worldwide — not just the Anglosphere.</p>

      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>{machineCount} machines catalogued</h3>
          <p>The catalogue maps {machineCount} machines — researched, classified, and queued for the
            curriculum. <strong>Catalogued is not the same as teachable.</strong> It's the map of where
            the work is headed, not a claim that all of it exists yet.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Four platforms teachable today</h3>
          <p>Complete on-ramps you can learn from now exist for four machines — the
            {launch.map((p, i) => (<><strong>{p.data.name}</strong>{i < launch.length - 1 ? ', ' : ''}</>))}.
            Each has a "Meet the Machine" primer and at least one finishable game.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>A family of tools</h3>
          <p>The curriculum sits inside the wider <a href="/family">198x family</a> — emulators
            (Emu198x), assemblers (Asm198x), asset cataloguing (Cat198x) and build tools (Build198x).
            Open-source siblings that share an origin, each with its own roadmap.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Original and enhanced</h3>
          <p>The curriculum teaches each system as it was, within its original constraints — and, where
            modern enhanced hardware exists (MEGA65, Spectrum Next, Vampire), how to use the extra room
            without losing the foundations.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ CREDIBILITY ============ -->
    <section class="band">
      <p class="aud">How it's built</p>
      <h2>In the open, with nothing hidden</h2>
      <div class="two">
        <div class="block">
          <h3>Free and openly licensed</h3>
          <p>Every page is free to read. The prose is Creative Commons (CC-BY); the code is MIT, public
            on GitHub. No ads, no paywall, no per-seat cost — and if the project ever stops, the content
            survives. One honest caveat: a couple of platforms need their original system ROMs to run,
            which aren't ours to give away.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="block">
          <h3>One person, AI as scaffolding</h3>
          <p>This is built by Steve Hill, a software engineer of nearly four decades, alongside a day
            job. Large language models help draft lessons and accelerate the work — declared plainly,
            because a project about seeing all the way down shouldn't hide its own workings. The aim is
            to lean on AI <em>less</em> over time as real experts contribute.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ REAL TODAY VS ROADMAP ============ -->
    <section class="band alt">
      <p class="aud">The honest line</p>
      <h2>What's real today, and what isn't yet</h2>
      <div class="two">
        <div class="block">
          <h3>Real today</h3>
          <ul class="ticks">
            <li>Four platforms with complete, finishable on-ramps</li>
            <li>BASIC and assembly paths where each machine had them</li>
            <li>The Vault, the Pattern Library, the Timeline and the Foundations</li>
            <li>A single assembler that builds every code sample on the site</li>
            <li>Everything public, open source, free to read and fork</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="block">
          <h3>Roadmap, not yet built</h3>
          <ul class="crosses">
            <li>The wider catalogue of machines — mapped, not yet written</li>
            <li>A polished, drop-in pack for classrooms</li>
            <li>The deferred siblings (a workbench, a media player)</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="note">There are no fixed unit or game counts to hit, and the curriculum is not
            currently verified on real hardware — that verification is suspended. The bar is on technique
            and quality, not quantity, and we'd rather show the gap than fabricate a destination.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ QUOTABLE FACTS ============ -->
    <section class="facts-band">
      <p class="aud">At a glance</p>
      <div class="facts">
        {facts.map(([n, label]) => (
          <div class="fact">
            <span class="fact-n">{n}</span>
            <span class="fact-label">{label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ MARKS ============ -->
    <section class="band" id="marks">
      <p class="aud">Using our name and logo</p>
      <h2>You do not need to ask.</h2>
      <p class="intro">Write about the project, talk about it, teach from it, link to it, say you
        built something with it. Reproduce the logo unaltered, at any size, to refer to us. That is
        what a mark is for, and it is the same test we apply to everybody else's marks when they
        appear in the <a href="/vault">Vault</a>.</p>
      <div class="marks-grid">
        <div class="marks-col">
          <p class="marks-h">Fine, unasked</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Articles, talks, videos, reading lists, links</li>
            <li>Saying you built something with it, or taught from it</li>
            <li>The logo unaltered, at any size</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="marks-col">
          <p class="marks-h">Not fine</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Implying we endorse, partnered with, or approved you</li>
            <li>Recolouring, restyling or cropping the mark</li>
            <li>Using it as your own branding or site furniture</li>
            <li>Any use suggesting we made your thing</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="foot">This is permission to refer to us, not a copyright licence on the artwork —
        a mark we could not withdraw from a misuse would not be much of a mark. If you want
        something outside this, Discord reaches a real person and the answer is usually yes.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ CONTACT ============ -->
    <section class="band">
      <p class="aud">Links and contact</p>
      <h2>Where to look, who to reach</h2>
      <p class="intro">The fullest statement of intent is the <a href="/about">About page</a>; the wider
        toolchain is on the <a href="/family">family page</a>. Everything happens in public:</p>
      <div class="links">
        <a class="link-card" href={ORG} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          <span class="link-t">GitHub</span>
          <span class="link-d">github.com/code198x — every repo, public</span>
        </a>
        <a class="link-card" href={DISCORD} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          <span class="link-t">Discord</span>
          <span class="link-d">The community, and the quickest way to reach me</span>
        </a>
        <a class="link-card" href={PATREON} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          <span class="link-t">Patreon</span>
          <span class="link-d">Support the work — you'll never pay to learn</span>
        </a>
        <a class="link-card" href="/rss.xml">
          <span class="link-t">RSS</span>
          <span class="link-d">Follow what ships, as it ships</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="foot">For anything not covered here — interviews, permissions, partnerships —
        <a href="/contribute">the contribution flow</a> explains how the work happens, and Discord
        reaches a real person.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .wrap { max-width: var(--container-max-width); margin: 0 auto; padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-6) var(--space-16); }
  .eyebrow { font-family: var(--font-family-mono); font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.16em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .mk { background: linear-gradient(180deg, transparent 58%, var(--color-house-tint) 58%); padding: 0 2px; }

  .vhead { margin-bottom: var(--space-10); }
  .vhead h1 { font-family: var(--font-family-read); font-weight: 700; font-size: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 4.8rem); line-height: 0.86;
      /* Reserve the descender the tight line-height does not: Literata's ink
         drops ~0.33em past the box, which collided with the strapline. */
      padding-bottom: 0.25em; margin: var(--space-1) 0 0; color: var(--color-text); }
  .herorule { height: 9px; width: 200px; max-width: 80%; background: var(--color-house); position: relative; margin: var(--space-3) 0 0; }
  .herorule::after { content: ""; position: absolute; right: 0; top: 0; bottom: 0; width: 24%; background: var(--color-house-2); }
  .lede { font-size: 1.05rem; max-width: 72ch; margin: var(--space-4) 0 0; color: var(--color-text); }

  /* Bordered bands — chunky shadow, square corners, house rhythm. */
  .band {
    border: 3px solid var(--color-text);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-chunky);
    padding: var(--space-8);
    margin-top: var(--space-10);
  }
  .band.alt { background: var(--color-bg-secondary); }

  .aud { font-family: var(--font-family-mono); font-size: 0.66rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin: 0; }
  .band h2 { font-family: var(--font-family-read); font-weight: 700; font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.8rem); line-height: 0.95; margin: var(--space-2) 0 0; color: var(--color-text); }
  .intro { font-size: 1rem; max-width: 72ch; margin: var(--space-3) 0 0; color: var(--color-text); }

  .cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-5); margin-top: var(--space-6); }
  .card { border: 3px solid var(--color-border); background: var(--color-surface); padding: var(--space-5); }
  .band.alt .card { background: var(--color-surface); }
  .card h3 { font-family: var(--font-family-mono); font-size: 0.95rem; font-weight: 800; margin: 0; color: var(--color-text); }
  .card p { font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.65; margin: var(--space-2) 0 0; }

  .two { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-5); margin-top: var(--space-6); }
  .block { border: 3px solid var(--color-border); background: var(--color-surface); padding: var(--space-5); }
  .block h3 { font-family: var(--font-family-mono); font-size: 0.95rem; font-weight: 800; margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); color: var(--color-text); }
  .block p { font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.65; margin: var(--space-2) 0 0; }

  .ticks, .crosses { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .ticks li, .crosses li { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.5; padding: 5px 0 5px 24px; position: relative; }
  .ticks li::before { content: "✓"; position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--color-house-2); font-weight: 700; }
  .crosses li::before { content: "○"; position: absolute; left: 0; color: var(--color-text-muted); font-family: var(--font-family-mono); }
  .block .note { font-size: 0.82rem; font-style: italic; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin: var(--space-4) 0 0; line-height: 1.6; }

  /* Quotable facts strip */
  .facts-band { margin-top: var(--space-10); }
  .facts { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: var(--space-4); margin-top: var(--space-3); }
  .fact { border: 3px solid var(--color-text); background: var(--color-surface); box-shadow: var(--shadow-chunky); padding: var(--space-5); }
  .fact-n { display: block; font-family: var(--font-family-read); font-weight: 700; font-size: 2.6rem; line-height: 0.9; color: var(--color-house-2); }
  .fact-label { display: block; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.5; margin-top: var(--space-2); }

  /* Contact links */
  .links { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-4); margin-top: var(--space-5); }
  .link-card { display: block; border: 3px solid var(--color-border); background: var(--color-surface); padding: var(--space-4); text-decoration: none; transition: transform var(--transition-base), box-shadow var(--transition-base), border-color var(--transition-base); }
  .link-card:hover, .link-card:focus-visible { transform: translate(-3px, -3px); box-shadow: var(--shadow-chunky); border-color: var(--color-house-2); }
  .link-t { display: block; font-family: var(--font-family-mono); font-size: 0.95rem; font-weight: 800; color: var(--ink); }
  .link-d { display: block; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin-top: 2px; line-height: 1.4; }

  .card a, .block a, .intro a, .foot a { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; border-bottom: 2px solid transparent; transition: border-color var(--transition-base); }
  .card a:hover, .block a:hover, .intro a:hover, .foot a:hover { border-bottom-color: var(--color-house-2); }
  .card strong, .block strong, .intro strong { color: var(--color-text); }

  .foot { margin-top: var(--space-5); font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--color-text-secondary); }

  /* Marks policy — two columns so "fine" and "not fine" are read together
     rather than as a list someone stops halfway down. */
  .marks-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-6); margin-top: var(--space-5); }
  .marks-h { font-family: var(--font-family-mono); font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-text-secondary); margin: 0 0 var(--space-2); }
  .marks-col ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; }
  .marks-col li { font-size: 0.94rem; margin-bottom: var(--space-1); }
  @media (max-width: 720px) { .marks-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .cards, .two, .facts, .links { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .band { padding: var(--space-6); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .facts { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  }
</style>
